  <div id="sec-all" class="section visible">
    <div class="cat-label">📋 Reporting Mechanisms</div>
    <div class="grid" id="grid-all-rep"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.12)">📄</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">FIR</div><div class="card-sub">First Information Report</div><span class="badge badge-blue">Legal</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The first official document filed with police when a cognizable (serious) offence is reported. It sets the entire criminal law machinery in motion and gives the case a legal identity.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_fir"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_fir">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_fir" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What it is</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Definition</span><span class="dval">A written document prepared by police based on the victim's oral or written complaint. Registered in a dedicated FIR register with a unique serial number.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Who files it</span><span class="dval">The victim, a witness, or a police officer who has knowledge of the offence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal basis</span><span class="dval">Section 154 CrPC (now Section 173 BNSS 2023). Police are legally bound to register — they cannot refuse.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Key contents</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fields</span><span class="dval">Date &amp; time, place of offence, name/address of complainant, description of offence, names of known accused, witnesses, stolen property.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Zero FIR</span><span class="dval">If offence occurs in a different jurisdiction, file a Zero FIR at any station — it is then transferred to the correct station.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">e-FIR</span><span class="dval">Many states allow online FIR filing. Cyber crime FIRs can be initiated via cybercrime.gov.in.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Why it matters</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Triggers investigation</span><span class="dval">Without an FIR, police cannot formally investigate, arrest suspects, or request CDR/IPDR from telecom companies.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bank freeze</span><span class="dval">An FIR number is required to request banks to freeze a fraudulent account under the golden-hour protocol.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Admissibility</span><span class="dval">FIR is admissible as evidence. Delay weakens the case — file within the golden hour (first hour after fraud).</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Pune resident received a call from a fake CBI officer and was coerced into transferring ₹3.5 lakh. She filed an FIR within 2 hours. Using the FIR, police froze the mule account before the money could be withdrawn.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Over 15.9 lakh cyber crime complaints were registered on cybercrime.gov.in between 2020–2023. Financial fraud was the top category.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.12)">🌐</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Complaint Portal</div><div class="card-sub">cybercrime.gov.in — National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal</div><span class="badge badge-blue">Reporting</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">India's government-run online portal (under MHA) where any citizen can report cyber crimes without visiting a police station. Specially fast-tracked for financial fraud cases.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_portal"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_portal">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_portal" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">About the portal</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">URL</span><span class="dval">cybercrime.gov.in (launched 2019, revamped 2023)</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Operated by</span><span class="dval">Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA), Government of India</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Coverage</span><span class="dval">Financial fraud, social media abuse, cyber stalking, ransomware, sextortion, BEC, and child abuse content.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How to report</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 1</span><span class="dval">Visit cybercrime.gov.in → click 'File a Complaint'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 2</span><span class="dval">Select category: Women/Child related crime OR Other Cyber Crimes</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 3</span><span class="dval">Fill personal details, describe the incident, upload screenshots/evidence</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 4</span><span class="dval">Submit — receive an acknowledgement/complaint ID for tracking</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Financial fraud</span><span class="dval">Use 'Report Financial Fraud' option for fastest response — linked to banks and payment gateways.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">After filing</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Routing</span><span class="dval">Complaint routed to relevant state cyber cell or district police.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bank alert</span><span class="dval">Banks alerted via Helpline 1930 to freeze accounts for financial fraud.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Timeline</span><span class="dval">Police must acknowledge within 24 hrs; action initiated within 48 hrs for financial cases.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Helpline</span><span class="dval">Call 1930 (Cyber Crime Helpline) for immediate assistance — works 24/7.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Delhi shopkeeper received a fake Google Pay QR code and lost ₹18,000. He reported on cybercrime.gov.in within 30 minutes. The portal flagged the UPI ID and the bank froze ₹12,000 before it was transferred further.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>The 1930 helpline received over 42 lakh calls in 2023 alone and has helped freeze fraudulent transactions worth hundreds of crores.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.12)">📢</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Cyber Tipline</div><div class="card-sub">Anonymous tip-based reporting system</div><span class="badge badge-blue">Reporting</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Dedicated channels for the public or insiders to anonymously report cyber offences — especially child exploitation, terrorism-related content, or critical infrastructure threats.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_tipline"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_tipline">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_tipline" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What it is</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Definition</span><span class="dval">A secure, often anonymous channel for reporting online illegal content or cyber threats — distinct from a formal FIR.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Who uses it</span><span class="dval">Concerned citizens, whistleblowers, industry researchers, content moderators, and NGOs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Types</span><span class="dval">CSAM, terrorism propaganda, cyber bullying, phishing campaigns, insider threats.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Major tiplines</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">NCMEC (USA)</span><span class="dval">National Center for Missing &amp; Exploited Children — global CyberTipline; millions of reports from Meta, Google, Snapchat annually.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CERT-In (India)</span><span class="dval">Indian Computer Emergency Response Team — tipline for critical infrastructure incidents, malware, phishing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">I4C (India)</span><span class="dval">Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre under MHA — coordinates state police, portal, and tipline responses.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Interpol</span><span class="dval">Accepts tips for transnational cybercrime through the ICSE database for child exploitation.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How tips are processed</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Triage</span><span class="dval">Analysts review each tip — spam and duplicates are filtered out.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Metadata</span><span class="dval">IP addresses, timestamps, device info submitted with tips are preserved as evidence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Forwarding</span><span class="dval">Actionable tips forwarded to relevant law enforcement with supporting technical data.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Anonymity</span><span class="dval">Reporter identity is protected under law; false tips can attract legal consequences.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A parent noticed their child receiving inappropriate messages on Instagram. They reported via NCMEC CyberTipline. NCMEC notified Indian law enforcement, who traced the account to a predator in Hyderabad using subscriber info and IPDR.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>NCMEC received over 32 million CyberTipline reports in 2022 — mostly from US-based tech platforms reporting CSAM detected on their servers.</p></div></div></div></div>
    <div class="cat-label">🔬 Evidence Types</div>
    <div class="grid" id="grid-all-ev"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">💻</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Digital Evidence</div><div class="card-sub">Electronically stored information with probative value</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Any data stored or transmitted in binary/digital form that can be used to prove or disprove facts in a legal proceeding — files, metadata, logs, emails, chat histories, and app data.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_digital-ev"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_digital-ev">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_digital-ev" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Files &amp; docs</span><span class="dval">Word docs, PDFs, spreadsheets, images — including deleted files recoverable via forensics.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Communication</span><span class="dval">Emails, SMS, WhatsApp chats, call logs, social media DMs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Logs</span><span class="dval">System logs, firewall logs, web server logs, application logs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Metadata</span><span class="dval">Hidden data inside files — author name, creation date, GPS coordinates in photos (EXIF data).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cloud data</span><span class="dval">Google Drive files, iCloud backups, OneDrive documents, email archives.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Preservation &amp; handling</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Hashing</span><span class="dval">Create MD5 or SHA-256 hash of the original drive. Any alteration changes the hash — proving tampering.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Write-blockers</span><span class="dval">Hardware/software tools that allow reading a drive without writing to it — preserving original state.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Chain of custody</span><span class="dval">Documented log of every person who handles evidence. Breaks in chain can render evidence inadmissible.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Imaging</span><span class="dval">Bit-for-bit forensic copy of the original media is analysed; original is sealed and stored.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal admissibility in India</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Section 65B</span><span class="dval">Indian Evidence Act (now BSA 2023) — requires a Section 65B certificate from the person responsible for the computer.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Requirements</span><span class="dval">Certificate must state: computer was in lawful use, stored info was regularly fed, computer was operating properly.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Supreme Court</span><span class="dval">Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao (2020) — SC ruled 65B certificate is mandatory for primary electronic evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In a corporate espionage case in Bengaluru, an employee was suspected of leaking trade secrets. Forensic investigators imaged his laptop, recovered deleted emails, and computed SHA-256 hashes for court. Metadata showed files were copied at 2 AM — contradicting his alibi.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Digital evidence now features in over 85% of all criminal cases globally. Even physical crimes like murder increasingly involve phone location data, ATM footage, and email trails.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">🔌</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Electronic Evidence</div><div class="card-sub">Evidence extracted from electronic devices</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Evidence obtained from electronic hardware — smartphones, computers, IoT devices, SIM cards, ATMs, and storage media — often requiring specialised forensic tools.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_elec-ev"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_elec-ev">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_elec-ev" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Sources</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Mobile phones</span><span class="dval">Call logs, SMS, apps, GPS history, photos, deleted WhatsApp messages, banking apps.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Computers</span><span class="dval">Browsing history, downloads, installed software, registry entries, temp files.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">SIM cards</span><span class="dval">Stored contacts, SMS, IMSI, ICCID — even after factory reset, SIM data persists.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IoT devices</span><span class="dval">Smart speakers store audio logs; smart TVs store browsing history; routers store connection logs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Vehicles</span><span class="dval">Modern ECUs record speed, braking, GPS routes, Bluetooth pairings — vital in hit-and-run cases.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Forensic tools</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cellebrite UFED</span><span class="dval">Industry standard for mobile extraction — extracts data from locked phones, deleted messages, app data.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">EnCase</span><span class="dval">Computer/disk forensics — creates forensic images and generates court-ready reports.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">FTK</span><span class="dval">Forensic Toolkit — parses hard drives, rebuilds deleted files, indexes all text for keyword search.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Oxygen Forensic</span><span class="dval">Specialised in cloud and app data — extracts Instagram DMs, Telegram messages, Google account data.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Order of volatility</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Rule</span><span class="dval">Collect the most volatile (quickly disappearing) data first.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Order</span><span class="dval">RAM → CPU cache → swap files → running processes → network connections → disk → removable media</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Why it matters</span><span class="dval">RAM contains encryption keys, active sessions, and passwords — all lost when device powers off.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In a sextortion case in Kerala, police seized the accused's Android phone. Using Cellebrite, they recovered 340 deleted video files proving the honey-trap operation — even after a factory reset. The IMEI matched CDR records confirming it was his device.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Cellebrite's tools can extract data from over 10,000 mobile device profiles. In India, Cellebrite is used by CBI, state forensic labs, and major state cyber cells.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">🔍</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Subscriber Information</div><div class="card-sub">Identity data linked to telecom/internet accounts</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Personal details held by telecom operators or ISPs for every SIM or internet account — name, address, KYC documents — linked to a mobile number or IP address.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_subscriber"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_subscriber">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_subscriber" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What it includes</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Personal data</span><span class="dval">Full legal name, residential address, date of birth, photograph.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">KYC documents</span><span class="dval">Aadhaar, PAN card, passport, or voter ID used at SIM activation.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Account details</span><span class="dval">Mobile number, SIM activation date, connection type (prepaid/postpaid), email ID.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMSI &amp; IMEI</span><span class="dval">IMSI (SIM identity) and IMEI (device identity) linked to the subscriber.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How investigators obtain it</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal process</span><span class="dval">Police issue a notice under Section 91 CrPC (Section 94 BNSS) to the TSP/ISP.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Urgent cases</span><span class="dval">For financial fraud or terrorism, requests go directly to the TSP's nodal officer.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Court order</span><span class="dval">For ISP-held IP-to-subscriber mapping, a magistrate order may be needed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Timeline</span><span class="dval">TSPs must respond within 7 days under TRAI; nodal officers handle urgent requests within 24 hrs.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fake KYC</span><span class="dval">Fraudsters use forged Aadhaar or purchased SIMs — subscriber data may not lead to real accused.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bulk SIM farms</span><span class="dval">Organised fraud groups use hundreds of SIMs bought with fake IDs from vulnerable populations.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">OTT services</span><span class="dval">WhatsApp and Telegram store minimal encrypted subscriber data — may require MLAT requests.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A victim received fraud calls from a number registered to a daily-wage worker in Bihar who had never owned a smartphone. Investigation revealed a SIM farm — hundreds of SIMs activated using the worker's Aadhaar without his knowledge, sold to a cyber fraud gang in Jamtara.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Jamtara, Jharkhand became infamous as India's 'phishing capital' — gangs used fake SIMs and simple scripts to commit crores in OTP fraud. The Netflix series 'Jamtara' is based on this.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">📞</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">CDR</div><div class="card-sub">Call Detail Record</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">A computerised log maintained by every telecom operator recording metadata of all voice calls and SMS — who called whom, when, for how long, and from where.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_cdr"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_cdr">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_cdr" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What CDR contains</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Call info</span><span class="dval">Originating number (A party), receiving number (B party), call start time, duration, call type.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Location</span><span class="dval">Cell tower ID (CGI) used during the call — triangulated to pinpoint approximate location.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMEI</span><span class="dval">Device IMEI linked to the SIM — crucial when suspects swap SIMs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Roaming</span><span class="dval">Roaming info if subscriber was in another circle — helps track movement across states.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types of CDR requests</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Targeted CDR</span><span class="dval">All calls made/received by a specific number over a time range.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Tower dump</span><span class="dval">All numbers active on a specific cell tower during a specific time window — used in kidnapping and murder cases.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMEI-based CDR</span><span class="dval">Track all SIMs ever used in a specific device — useful when SIM is discarded but phone is retained.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Reverse lookup</span><span class="dval">Find all calls to/from a victim's number — identifies unknown callers.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal aspects</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Retention</span><span class="dval">TRAI mandates telecom operators retain CDR for minimum 1 year.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Request process</span><span class="dval">Senior police officer (DSP and above) can request CDR directly from telecom nodal officers.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Admissibility</span><span class="dval">CDR requires a Section 65B certificate for court submission.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In the 2012 Delhi gang rape case, CDR analysis of all phones near the crime scene and the bus route helped police reconstruct movements of all six accused. Tower dump data from three towers narrowed down suspects within hours.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>A single CDR request can return thousands of records. Investigators use tools like i2 Analyst's Notebook or Maltego to visualise call networks and identify 'hub' numbers in a fraud gang.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">🌐</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">IPDR</div><div class="card-sub">Internet Protocol Detail Record</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The internet counterpart of CDR — every ISP/TSP logs all data sessions: source IP, destination IP, ports, protocol, data volume, and timestamps. Critical for tracing internet-based crimes.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_ipdr"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_ipdr">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_ipdr" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What IPDR contains</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Session data</span><span class="dval">Source IP, destination IP, source port, destination port, protocol (TCP/UDP), timestamp.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Volume</span><span class="dval">Amount of data uploaded/downloaded per session — can indicate large file transfers.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Application</span><span class="dval">Some IPDRs include app-layer info (WhatsApp, YouTube) under deep packet inspection.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">NAT port</span><span class="dval">In CGNAT environments, port number uniquely identifies the subscriber sharing a public IP.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How it is used</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IP-to-person mapping</span><span class="dval">Police get IP from server log → request IPDR from ISP → identify which subscriber used that IP at that time.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Transaction trail</span><span class="dval">Identify which internet connection was used to perform a fraudulent UPI transaction or email hack.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cross-verification</span><span class="dval">Correlate IPDR with CDR — verify if same person was on a data session and call simultaneously.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Dark web</span><span class="dval">If suspect accessed .onion sites via Tor, IPDR shows encrypted sessions to known Tor entry nodes.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal aspects</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal basis</span><span class="dval">Obtained under Section 69 IT Act / Section 91 CrPC (Section 94 BNSS) notice to ISP.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CGNAT challenge</span><span class="dval">Thousands of users share one public IP — port number in IPDR is essential to identify one subscriber.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Retention</span><span class="dval">DoT guidelines require ISPs to retain IPDR for 1 year.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A ransomware attack hit a Mumbai hospital. The server log showed attacker IP: 103.45.XX.XX. Police served an IPDR request to the ISP, which mapped that IP and port to a specific broadband subscriber in Thane. Combined with CDR, they confirmed the suspect was home during the attack.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>The Supreme Court and High Courts have upheld IPDR as admissible evidence in India when combined with proper 65B certification.</p></div></div></div></div>
    <div class="cat-label">🧠 Intelligence Methods</div>
    <div class="grid" id="grid-all-intel"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🔎</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">OSINT</div><div class="card-sub">Open Source Intelligence</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Intelligence collected from publicly available sources — social media, websites, domain records, satellite imagery, public databases, and dark web forums — without any covert access.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_osint"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_osint">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_osint" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Sources</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Social media</span><span class="dval">Facebook, Instagram, X (Twitter), LinkedIn — profile photos, check-ins, employer info, relationships.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">WHOIS &amp; DNS</span><span class="dval">Domain registration records, nameservers, IP history — reveals who registered a phishing domain.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Shodan</span><span class="dval">Search engine for internet-connected devices — finds exposed servers, cameras, industrial systems.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Dark web</span><span class="dval">Forums like BreachForums — leaked databases, advertised malware, criminal profiles.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Geo-intelligence</span><span class="dval">Google Maps, satellite images, Wikimapia — verify locations claimed by suspects.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">OSINT tools</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Maltego</span><span class="dval">Visual link analysis — maps relationships between domains, IPs, emails, and social profiles.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">SpiderFoot</span><span class="dval">Automated OSINT scanner — discovers emails, subdomains, breached credentials linked to a target.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">theHarvester</span><span class="dval">Collects emails, hostnames, IPs associated with a domain from search engines.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Google Dorks</span><span class="dval">Advanced search operators (site:, filetype:, intitle:) to find exposed data on public websites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Reverse image</span><span class="dval">TinEye or Google Images — identify fake profile photos stolen from another person.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal &amp; ethical limits</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Allowed</span><span class="dval">Viewing publicly shared content, domain records, open government data, public social media profiles.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">NOT allowed</span><span class="dval">Creating fake profiles to befriend a suspect, hacking, or accessing private data without authorisation.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Documentation</span><span class="dval">Screenshots with timestamps and URLs; archive web pages using HTTrack as evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Kolkata woman was blackmailed by someone claiming to have her private photos. Investigators found the blackmailer's WhatsApp profile photo on a Nigerian Instagram account. Reverse image search led to a network of 12 fake profiles used for honey-trap operations across India.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>OSINT was central to identifying perpetrators of the 2021 Colonial Pipeline ransomware attack. Analysts traced Bitcoin wallets in the ransom note to DarkSide's infrastructure through blockchain OSINT.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🕵️</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">HUMINT</div><div class="card-sub">Human Intelligence</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Intelligence gathered from human sources — informants, undercover officers, interviews with suspects and victims, and inter-agency cooperation — as opposed to technical or open-source methods.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_humint"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_humint">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_humint" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Methods</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Informants</span><span class="dval">Recruited individuals inside criminal networks who provide tips in exchange for protection or money.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Undercover ops</span><span class="dval">Officers infiltrate online criminal forums posing as buyers, hackers, or money mules.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Interviews</span><span class="dval">Structured questioning of victims, witnesses, and arrested suspects to extract operational details.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Debriefing</span><span class="dval">Arrested cybercriminals cooperate in exchange for plea deals — revealing gang structure, scripts, bank accounts.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Inter-agency</span><span class="dval">Coordination between CBI, state police, CERT-In, banks, and international agencies (Interpol, FBI).</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">HUMINT in cyber crime</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Forum infiltration</span><span class="dval">Officers join dark web forums posing as buyers to identify sellers of malware, stolen cards, or drugs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Call centre raids</span><span class="dval">HUMINT from disgruntled employees exposes fake tech-support or loan-app fraud call centres.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Courier networks</span><span class="dval">Money mule recruiters operate physical courier networks — informants inside logistics companies help.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cross-border</span><span class="dval">Interpol's Gateway programme and bilateral MLAT agreements are formal HUMINT cooperation channels.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cover maintenance</span><span class="dval">Undercover officers must maintain credibility over months without breaking cover.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal constraints</span><span class="dval">Evidence from undercover operations must comply with entrapment laws — officer cannot induce a crime.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Source reliability</span><span class="dval">Informants may have personal agendas; information must be corroborated with technical evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In Operation Chakra-II (2023), CBI and state police used HUMINT from informants inside telecom stores plus OSINT to dismantle a network handling over 1 crore fraudulent SIM cards. HUMINT from an arrested mule revealed the gang's operator in Rajasthan.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Europol's 2023 IOCTA report credits HUMINT-based infiltration of dark web forums as the primary method for identifying ransomware-as-a-service administrators — technical attribution alone is not sufficient.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🎭</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Threat Actor</div><div class="card-sub">Entity responsible for a cyber attack</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Any individual, group, or nation-state that intentionally carries out malicious cyber activities — from bored teenagers to state-sponsored operatives — categorised by motive, capability, and sophistication.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_threat-actor"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_threat-actor">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_threat-actor" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Script kiddies</span><span class="dval">Low-skill individuals using pre-written tools without understanding them. Motive: thrill/attention.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Hacktivists</span><span class="dval">Groups like Anonymous hacking for political/social causes — DDoS attacks, data leaks.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cybercriminals</span><span class="dval">Financially motivated individuals or organised gangs — ransomware, BEC, card fraud, phishing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Insider threats</span><span class="dval">Disgruntled employees, contractors, or bribed insiders misusing legitimate access.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Nation-state APTs</span><span class="dval">Government-backed threat groups — Lazarus (North Korea), APT28 (Russia), APT41 (China).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cyber mercenaries</span><span class="dval">Hack-for-hire groups like NSO Group (Pegasus) working for paying clients.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Profiling an actor</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">TTPs</span><span class="dval">Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures — the actor's unique modus operandi, mapped using MITRE ATT&amp;CK.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Infrastructure</span><span class="dval">C2 server IP ranges, hosting providers, domain patterns, SSL certificate reuse.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Malware signatures</span><span class="dval">Custom malware carries code artifacts, strings, or compile paths that identify the developer.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Timing patterns</span><span class="dval">Attack timestamps reveal the actor's timezone and work schedule.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">What it is</span><span class="dval">Globally accepted knowledge base of adversary tactics based on real-world observations.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Use in India</span><span class="dval">Used by CERT-In, NIC, and large banks to map attacks to known threat actors.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">14 tactics</span><span class="dval">Reconnaissance → Initial Access → Execution → Persistence → Privilege Escalation → … → Exfiltration → Impact</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>The 2020 Mumbai power grid outage was linked by Recorded Future to RedEcho, a suspected Chinese state-sponsored APT group, based on infrastructure overlaps and TTPs matching previous Chinese cyber operations.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>North Korea's Lazarus Group is estimated to have stolen over $3 billion in cryptocurrency between 2017–2023 — funding the country's weapons programme. They are the most prolific financially motivated nation-state actor.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🧬</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Attribution</div><div class="card-sub">Identifying the perpetrator of a cyber attack</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The complex process of linking a cyber attack to a specific person, group, or nation-state using technical indicators, behavioural analysis, OSINT, HUMINT, and legal-grade evidence.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_attribution"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_attribution">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_attribution" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Levels of attribution</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Technical</span><span class="dval">Lowest level — identifies IP, domain, malware hash. Easily spoofed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Operational</span><span class="dval">Links multiple incidents through shared infrastructure, TTPs, and timing — more reliable.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Strategic</span><span class="dval">Identifies actor's long-term goals, geopolitical context, motive — involves intelligence agencies.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal</span><span class="dval">Highest standard — proof beyond reasonable doubt required for criminal prosecution.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Technical indicators</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IP &amp; domain history</span><span class="dval">Passive DNS, WHOIS history, Shodan — has this IP been used in previous attacks?</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Malware analysis</span><span class="dval">Code similarity, compile-time timezone, hardcoded strings, mutex names, PDB paths.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">C2 infrastructure</span><span class="dval">Reuse of hosting providers, SSL certificates, URL patterns across campaigns.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Victimology</span><span class="dval">Who was targeted? Nation-state actors target specific sectors/countries, revealing motive.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">False flags</span><span class="dval">Sophisticated actors plant evidence pointing to another group.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">VPN / Tor / proxies</span><span class="dval">Multi-hop anonymisation makes IP-based attribution unreliable.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Shared tools</span><span class="dval">Multiple groups use Cobalt Strike or Metasploit — tool-based attribution alone is insufficient.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Speed vs accuracy</span><span class="dval">Intelligence agencies attribute quickly for policy; legal prosecution requires far stronger evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>The 2021 SolarWinds attack was attributed to APT29 (Russia's SVR) based on code overlap with previous Turla malware, Eastern European infrastructure, US government victimology, and timestamps consistent with Moscow business hours.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Only about 20% of cyber attacks result in successful legal attribution (arrest + conviction). Nation-state attacks almost never result in prosecution — geopolitics prevent extradition.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🔌</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Takedown</div><div class="card-sub">Disabling malicious online infrastructure</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The coordinated action of disabling, seizing, or disrupting malicious online infrastructure — phishing sites, C2 servers, botnets, or dark web marketplaces — through legal orders or technical means.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_takedown"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_takedown">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_takedown" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Domain suspension</span><span class="dval">Registrar suspends domain after abuse complaint or court order — most common for phishing sites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Server seizure</span><span class="dval">Physical or virtual server seized by law enforcement — data preserved as evidence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">ISP blocking</span><span class="dval">ISP instructed to block access to malicious IP/domain within their network.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Sink-holing</span><span class="dval">DNS of botnet's C2 domain redirected to law enforcement server — bots connect harmlessly.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Dark web takedown</span><span class="dval">Coordinated international operation seizes servers hosting illegal marketplace.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Process in India</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CERT-In</span><span class="dval">Issues advisory to hosting providers and ISPs — typically within 4 hours for critical sites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Court order</span><span class="dval">For persistent sites, police obtain court order under IT Act Section 69A.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">DOT blocking</span><span class="dval">Department of Telecom can block at network level — used for fraud, terror-related sites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">International</span><span class="dval">India uses Interpol channels, MLAT, or direct coordination with APNIC/ICANN for foreign-hosted sites.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bulletproof hosting</span><span class="dval">Providers in certain jurisdictions that ignore abuse complaints.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fast-flux DNS</span><span class="dval">Malware rapidly changes IP addresses — traditional domain blocking is ineffective.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Hydra effect</span><span class="dval">When one site is taken down, five more appear — requires sustained disruption.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>Operation Endgame (May 2024, Europol) was the largest-ever botnet takedown — simultaneously disrupting Smokeloader, IcedID, Trickbot, and SystemBC across 100+ servers in Europe and North America. Over 2,000 domains were sink-holed.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Silk Road was taken down by FBI in 2013. Its founder was identified partly because he posted about the site using his real email on a public forum — a classic OSINT failure.</p></div></div></div></div>
    <div class="cat-label">⚠️ Cyber Crime Types</div>
    <div class="grid" id="grid-all-cr"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">💸</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Money Mule</div><div class="card-sub">Recruited fund launderer for cyber criminals</div><span class="badge badge-red">Financial crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">A person — often unsuspecting — who receives stolen money into their bank account and transfers it onward minus a commission. They form the crucial cash-out layer in almost every financial cyber crime.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_mule"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_mule">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_mule" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How mules are recruited</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fake job ads</span><span class="dval">Ads for 'payment processor', 'financial agent', or 'remote work' — no skills required, high pay promised.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Social media DMs</span><span class="dval">Instagram/WhatsApp messages offering part-time income for 'helping process payments'.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Romance scams</span><span class="dval">Romantic partner built online over months gradually convinces victim to receive/forward money.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Loan app fraud</span><span class="dval">Victims in debt coerced into mule activity to 'repay loans' to fraudulent apps.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How the operation works</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Layer 1</span><span class="dval">Fraud proceeds enter mule's account (usually within minutes of the scam).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Transfer</span><span class="dval">Mule transfers to another mule account or crypto exchange — keeping 5–20% commission.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Layering</span><span class="dval">Multiple mules used in sequence to distance money from origin.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cash out</span><span class="dval">Final mule withdraws cash, buys crypto, or purchases gift cards for the gang.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal consequences</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">India</span><span class="dval">Prosecution under IPC 420 (cheating), PMLA, and IT Act Section 66.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Sentence</span><span class="dval">PMLA conviction carries 3–7 years imprisonment and attachment of property.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">No ignorance defence</span><span class="dval">Receiving suspicious funds and forwarding them constitutes wilful blindness — ignorance is not a full defence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Account freeze</span><span class="dval">Innocent mule accounts are frozen — victim loses access to own funds for months.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A 22-year-old engineering student in Pune responded to a WhatsApp job ad offering ₹500 per transfer. He forwarded ₹4.2 lakh across 3 transactions. Police traced fraud proceeds to his account via IPDR. He was arrested under PMLA despite claiming ignorance, and his account was frozen for 18 months.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Europol's 2023 EMMA operation identified 10,000 money mules across Europe in a single month-long operation.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">₿</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Cryptocurrency Tracing</div><div class="card-sub">Blockchain forensic analysis to follow illicit funds</div><span class="badge badge-red">Financial crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The discipline of tracking cryptocurrency transactions across public blockchains to identify wallets, exchanges, and the real-world identities behind illicit funds — despite the pseudonymous nature of crypto.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_crypto"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_crypto">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_crypto" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Why crypto is traceable</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Public ledger</span><span class="dval">Every Bitcoin/Ethereum transaction is permanently recorded on a public blockchain — anyone can view it.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Pseudonymous</span><span class="dval">Wallets are addresses with no name, but all transactions are visible. Link address to identity = game over.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Clustering</span><span class="dval">If two addresses fund the same transaction, they are likely controlled by the same person.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Exchange KYC</span><span class="dval">When funds reach a regulated exchange (Binance, WazirX), KYC data links wallet to real person.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Obfuscation &amp; counters</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Mixers/tumblers</span><span class="dval">Pools users' crypto and returns different coins — breaks direct transaction trail.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Coinjoin</span><span class="dval">Multiple users combine transactions to obscure who paid whom.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Chain-hopping</span><span class="dval">Convert Bitcoin → Monero → Ethereum across chains to obscure trail.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Monero</span><span class="dval">Privacy coin — transactions not publicly visible. Much harder to trace.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Counter tools</span><span class="dval">Chainalysis Reactor can de-mix transactions using statistical analysis and exchange data.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Investigative tools</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Chainalysis Reactor</span><span class="dval">Used by CBI, ED, and major international agencies — visualises transaction flows across blockchains.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Elliptic Navigator</span><span class="dval">Strong on DeFi and NFT fraud tracing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CipherTrace</span><span class="dval">Acquired by Mastercard — used for exchange compliance and law enforcement.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Open source</span><span class="dval">Blockchair, OXT.me, Etherscan — free block explorers for basic transaction tracing.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>After the 2022 Bitfinex hack ($4.5B in Bitcoin stolen in 2016), IRS-CI and DOJ traced funds through 12 layers of mixers over 6 years using Chainalysis. The suspects were arrested when funds finally moved to a KYC exchange.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>The US DOJ seized over $1.4 billion in Bitcoin from the Bitfinex hack — the largest financial seizure in DOJ history. The blockchain's permanent ledger meant 6-year-old transactions were still fully traceable.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">📱</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">SIM Swap Fraud</div><div class="card-sub">Unauthorised mobile number porting to a criminal's SIM</div><span class="badge badge-red">Telecom crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Fraudsters convince or bribe a telecom outlet to port the victim's mobile number to a new SIM they control — giving them access to all OTPs, calls, and 2FA messages linked to the number.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_simswap"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_simswap">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_simswap" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How it happens</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">PII collection</span><span class="dval">Attacker collects victim's personal details from data breaches or phishing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Outlet approach</span><span class="dval">Attacker visits a telecom store with forged ID or uses a corrupt insider to request a duplicate SIM.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bypass verification</span><span class="dval">Agent asks KYC questions; attacker answers using stolen PII — 'What is your registered address?'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">SIM activated</span><span class="dval">New SIM issued; victim's original SIM deactivated. Attacker receives all calls and OTPs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bank access</span><span class="dval">Attacker logs into net banking with stolen credentials + OTP from new SIM → transfers all funds.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Warning signs for victims</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Signal loss</span><span class="dval">Phone suddenly shows 'No network' or 'SIM not registered' — first and most critical sign.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Missed bank alerts</span><span class="dval">Stop receiving OTPs or bank SMS — attacker is receiving them.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Unusual calls</span><span class="dval">Incoming calls asking you to confirm OTPs you didn't request.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Immediate action</span><span class="dval">Call your telecom operator on another phone immediately; ask for SIM freeze. Call bank to freeze account.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Investigative approach</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CDR analysis</span><span class="dval">Check which tower new SIM connected to first — locates where the fraudster activated it.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Outlet CCTV</span><span class="dval">Most outlets have CCTV — footage of person who requested the duplicate SIM.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Outlet records</span><span class="dval">Biometric/signature records of who submitted the duplicate SIM request.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Insider angle</span><span class="dval">If insider is involved, CDR between insider and attacker is traced via call records.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Mumbai stock broker lost ₹1.6 crore in 4 hours after a SIM swap. His number was ported after an attacker visited a franchise with a forged Aadhaar. The attacker used stolen net banking credentials + OTPs from the new SIM to make 8 transfers to mule accounts.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>RBI's 2023 Annual Report listed SIM swap fraud as one of the top 3 banking fraud methods in India. TRAI has since mandated a 7-day cooling period for SIM re-issuance.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">⚠️</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Sextortion</div><div class="card-sub">Online sexual blackmail using intimate content</div><span class="badge badge-red">Sexual crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Criminals use real or fabricated intimate images/videos to blackmail victims for money or more content — often starting with a honey-trap on social media or dating apps.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_sextortion"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_sextortion">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_sextortion" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Common methods</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Honey-trap</span><span class="dval">Fake attractive profile befriends target → builds trust → convinces to video call → records compromising content.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Malware variant</span><span class="dval">Victim's device infected with RAT → webcam silently activated → footage captured.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Non-consensual</span><span class="dval">Ex-partner or acquaintance threatens to share real intimate content unless demands are met.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">AI deepfake</span><span class="dval">Attacker uses victim's social media photos to create deepfake intimate content — no real contact needed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Teen targeting</span><span class="dval">Targets minors on gaming platforms — grooming leads to obtaining images, then blackmail.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Demands &amp; escalation</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Financial</span><span class="dval">Money via UPI, crypto, or gift cards — typically ₹5,000 to ₹5 lakh.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">More content</span><span class="dval">Demands escalate — victim pressured to send more images to 'buy silence'.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Paying backfires</span><span class="dval">Payment almost always leads to more demands — attacker knows victim will comply.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What victims should do</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Do NOT pay</span><span class="dval">Payment escalates demands and confirms victim will comply.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Preserve evidence</span><span class="dval">Screenshot all conversations, note profile links, record UPI/account details.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Report</span><span class="dval">cybercrime.gov.in → 'Women/Child related crime' → Sextortion. Also call 1930.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Platform report</span><span class="dval">Report the account — most platforms have emergency removal for non-consensual intimate images (NCII).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Support</span><span class="dval">Contact iCall (9152987821) or Vandrevala Foundation for psychological support.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A 17-year-old student in Chennai was contacted by a 'girl' on Instagram. After weeks of conversation she was convinced to video call. The call was recorded and she was threatened unless she paid ₹20,000. She reported to cybercrime.gov.in; police traced the gang to Rajasthan running 50+ simultaneous operations.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>NCRB data shows sextortion cases in India grew 75% between 2020–2022. Bharatpur, Rajasthan became known as the 'sextortion capital' — police have dismantled dozens of gangs there.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">📧</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">BEC</div><div class="card-sub">Business Email Compromise</div><span class="badge badge-red">Corporate crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">A sophisticated scam targeting businesses — attackers compromise or spoof a corporate email to deceive employees, vendors, or clients into transferring money or sharing sensitive data.</div><div class="expand-btn open" id="btn_all_bec"><span class="arr" id="arr_all_bec">▲</span>&nbsp;Collapse</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_all_bec" style="display: block;"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types of BEC</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CEO fraud</span><span class="dval">Attacker impersonates the CEO and emails CFO: 'Transfer ₹50 lakh urgently — confidential deal.'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Invoice fraud</span><span class="dval">Attacker spoofs a vendor's email: 'Our bank account has changed — please send payment to new account.'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Attorney impersonation</span><span class="dval">Fake law firm email claims victim owes money or must act urgently on a legal matter.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">HR/W-2 fraud</span><span class="dval">Attacker impersonates employee to HR: 'Please update my salary transfer to this new account.'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Real estate</span><span class="dval">Buyer receives spoofed email from 'lawyer' with new bank account for property down payment.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How attackers gain access</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Phishing</span><span class="dval">Credential-harvesting email tricks employee into entering password on a fake login page.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Credential stuffing</span><span class="dval">Breached password from another site tried on corporate email — works if employees reuse passwords.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMAP brute force</span><span class="dval">Automated attack guesses email password — especially on accounts without MFA enabled.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Email spoofing</span><span class="dval">Attacker forges the 'From' field to look like CEO — no actual hack needed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Domain lookalike</span><span class="dval">Registers company-rnail.com (rn looks like m) — victim doesn't notice the difference.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Red flags &amp; prevention</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Urgency + secrecy</span><span class="dval">Any email demanding urgent wire transfer with 'keep this confidential' is a major red flag.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Reply-to mismatch</span><span class="dval">Display name says 'CEO' but reply-to is ceo@company-rnail.com — always check the actual email address.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Verification</span><span class="dval">Call the sender on a known number to verify any payment instruction.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">MFA</span><span class="dval">Enable multi-factor authentication on all corporate email accounts.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">DMARC/SPF</span><span class="dval">Configure email authentication protocols to prevent domain spoofing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Recovery</span><span class="dval">Report to cybercrime.gov.in immediately; call 1930; contact bank to issue SWIFT recall.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Hyderabad manufacturing company received an email from its Singapore supplier saying their bank account had changed. Finance transferred $85,000 to the new account. Investigation revealed the supplier's email was hacked 3 weeks earlier; the attacker waited silently until a large invoice was expected.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>FBI IC3 data: BEC caused $2.9 billion in losses in the USA alone in 2023 — the single highest-loss cybercrime category, far exceeding ransomware. Globally, BEC losses are estimated at $50+ billion since 2013.</p></div></div></div></div>
  </div>

  <div id="sec-reporting" class="section"><div class="grid" id="grid-reporting"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.12)">📄</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">FIR</div><div class="card-sub">First Information Report</div><span class="badge badge-blue">Legal</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The first official document filed with police when a cognizable (serious) offence is reported. It sets the entire criminal law machinery in motion and gives the case a legal identity.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_rep_fir"><span class="arr" id="arr_rep_fir">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_rep_fir"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What it is</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Definition</span><span class="dval">A written document prepared by police based on the victim's oral or written complaint. Registered in a dedicated FIR register with a unique serial number.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Who files it</span><span class="dval">The victim, a witness, or a police officer who has knowledge of the offence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal basis</span><span class="dval">Section 154 CrPC (now Section 173 BNSS 2023). Police are legally bound to register — they cannot refuse.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Key contents</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fields</span><span class="dval">Date &amp; time, place of offence, name/address of complainant, description of offence, names of known accused, witnesses, stolen property.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Zero FIR</span><span class="dval">If offence occurs in a different jurisdiction, file a Zero FIR at any station — it is then transferred to the correct station.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">e-FIR</span><span class="dval">Many states allow online FIR filing. Cyber crime FIRs can be initiated via cybercrime.gov.in.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Why it matters</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Triggers investigation</span><span class="dval">Without an FIR, police cannot formally investigate, arrest suspects, or request CDR/IPDR from telecom companies.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bank freeze</span><span class="dval">An FIR number is required to request banks to freeze a fraudulent account under the golden-hour protocol.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Admissibility</span><span class="dval">FIR is admissible as evidence. Delay weakens the case — file within the golden hour (first hour after fraud).</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Pune resident received a call from a fake CBI officer and was coerced into transferring ₹3.5 lakh. She filed an FIR within 2 hours. Using the FIR, police froze the mule account before the money could be withdrawn.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Over 15.9 lakh cyber crime complaints were registered on cybercrime.gov.in between 2020–2023. Financial fraud was the top category.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.12)">🌐</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Complaint Portal</div><div class="card-sub">cybercrime.gov.in — National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal</div><span class="badge badge-blue">Reporting</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">India's government-run online portal (under MHA) where any citizen can report cyber crimes without visiting a police station. Specially fast-tracked for financial fraud cases.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_rep_portal"><span class="arr" id="arr_rep_portal">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_rep_portal"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">About the portal</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">URL</span><span class="dval">cybercrime.gov.in (launched 2019, revamped 2023)</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Operated by</span><span class="dval">Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA), Government of India</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Coverage</span><span class="dval">Financial fraud, social media abuse, cyber stalking, ransomware, sextortion, BEC, and child abuse content.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How to report</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 1</span><span class="dval">Visit cybercrime.gov.in → click 'File a Complaint'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 2</span><span class="dval">Select category: Women/Child related crime OR Other Cyber Crimes</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 3</span><span class="dval">Fill personal details, describe the incident, upload screenshots/evidence</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Step 4</span><span class="dval">Submit — receive an acknowledgement/complaint ID for tracking</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Financial fraud</span><span class="dval">Use 'Report Financial Fraud' option for fastest response — linked to banks and payment gateways.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">After filing</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Routing</span><span class="dval">Complaint routed to relevant state cyber cell or district police.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bank alert</span><span class="dval">Banks alerted via Helpline 1930 to freeze accounts for financial fraud.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Timeline</span><span class="dval">Police must acknowledge within 24 hrs; action initiated within 48 hrs for financial cases.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Helpline</span><span class="dval">Call 1930 (Cyber Crime Helpline) for immediate assistance — works 24/7.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Delhi shopkeeper received a fake Google Pay QR code and lost ₹18,000. He reported on cybercrime.gov.in within 30 minutes. The portal flagged the UPI ID and the bank froze ₹12,000 before it was transferred further.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>The 1930 helpline received over 42 lakh calls in 2023 alone and has helped freeze fraudulent transactions worth hundreds of crores.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(59,130,246,0.12)">📢</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Cyber Tipline</div><div class="card-sub">Anonymous tip-based reporting system</div><span class="badge badge-blue">Reporting</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Dedicated channels for the public or insiders to anonymously report cyber offences — especially child exploitation, terrorism-related content, or critical infrastructure threats.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_rep_tipline"><span class="arr" id="arr_rep_tipline">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_rep_tipline"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What it is</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Definition</span><span class="dval">A secure, often anonymous channel for reporting online illegal content or cyber threats — distinct from a formal FIR.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Who uses it</span><span class="dval">Concerned citizens, whistleblowers, industry researchers, content moderators, and NGOs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Types</span><span class="dval">CSAM, terrorism propaganda, cyber bullying, phishing campaigns, insider threats.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Major tiplines</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">NCMEC (USA)</span><span class="dval">National Center for Missing &amp; Exploited Children — global CyberTipline; millions of reports from Meta, Google, Snapchat annually.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CERT-In (India)</span><span class="dval">Indian Computer Emergency Response Team — tipline for critical infrastructure incidents, malware, phishing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">I4C (India)</span><span class="dval">Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre under MHA — coordinates state police, portal, and tipline responses.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Interpol</span><span class="dval">Accepts tips for transnational cybercrime through the ICSE database for child exploitation.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How tips are processed</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Triage</span><span class="dval">Analysts review each tip — spam and duplicates are filtered out.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Metadata</span><span class="dval">IP addresses, timestamps, device info submitted with tips are preserved as evidence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Forwarding</span><span class="dval">Actionable tips forwarded to relevant law enforcement with supporting technical data.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Anonymity</span><span class="dval">Reporter identity is protected under law; false tips can attract legal consequences.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A parent noticed their child receiving inappropriate messages on Instagram. They reported via NCMEC CyberTipline. NCMEC notified Indian law enforcement, who traced the account to a predator in Hyderabad using subscriber info and IPDR.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>NCMEC received over 32 million CyberTipline reports in 2022 — mostly from US-based tech platforms reporting CSAM detected on their servers.</p></div></div></div></div></div>
  <div id="sec-evidence" class="section"><div class="grid" id="grid-evidence"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">💻</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Digital Evidence</div><div class="card-sub">Electronically stored information with probative value</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Any data stored or transmitted in binary/digital form that can be used to prove or disprove facts in a legal proceeding — files, metadata, logs, emails, chat histories, and app data.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_ev_digital-ev"><span class="arr" id="arr_ev_digital-ev">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_ev_digital-ev"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Files &amp; docs</span><span class="dval">Word docs, PDFs, spreadsheets, images — including deleted files recoverable via forensics.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Communication</span><span class="dval">Emails, SMS, WhatsApp chats, call logs, social media DMs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Logs</span><span class="dval">System logs, firewall logs, web server logs, application logs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Metadata</span><span class="dval">Hidden data inside files — author name, creation date, GPS coordinates in photos (EXIF data).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cloud data</span><span class="dval">Google Drive files, iCloud backups, OneDrive documents, email archives.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Preservation &amp; handling</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Hashing</span><span class="dval">Create MD5 or SHA-256 hash of the original drive. Any alteration changes the hash — proving tampering.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Write-blockers</span><span class="dval">Hardware/software tools that allow reading a drive without writing to it — preserving original state.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Chain of custody</span><span class="dval">Documented log of every person who handles evidence. Breaks in chain can render evidence inadmissible.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Imaging</span><span class="dval">Bit-for-bit forensic copy of the original media is analysed; original is sealed and stored.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal admissibility in India</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Section 65B</span><span class="dval">Indian Evidence Act (now BSA 2023) — requires a Section 65B certificate from the person responsible for the computer.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Requirements</span><span class="dval">Certificate must state: computer was in lawful use, stored info was regularly fed, computer was operating properly.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Supreme Court</span><span class="dval">Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Kushanrao (2020) — SC ruled 65B certificate is mandatory for primary electronic evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In a corporate espionage case in Bengaluru, an employee was suspected of leaking trade secrets. Forensic investigators imaged his laptop, recovered deleted emails, and computed SHA-256 hashes for court. Metadata showed files were copied at 2 AM — contradicting his alibi.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Digital evidence now features in over 85% of all criminal cases globally. Even physical crimes like murder increasingly involve phone location data, ATM footage, and email trails.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">🔌</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Electronic Evidence</div><div class="card-sub">Evidence extracted from electronic devices</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Evidence obtained from electronic hardware — smartphones, computers, IoT devices, SIM cards, ATMs, and storage media — often requiring specialised forensic tools.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_ev_elec-ev"><span class="arr" id="arr_ev_elec-ev">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_ev_elec-ev"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Sources</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Mobile phones</span><span class="dval">Call logs, SMS, apps, GPS history, photos, deleted WhatsApp messages, banking apps.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Computers</span><span class="dval">Browsing history, downloads, installed software, registry entries, temp files.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">SIM cards</span><span class="dval">Stored contacts, SMS, IMSI, ICCID — even after factory reset, SIM data persists.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IoT devices</span><span class="dval">Smart speakers store audio logs; smart TVs store browsing history; routers store connection logs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Vehicles</span><span class="dval">Modern ECUs record speed, braking, GPS routes, Bluetooth pairings — vital in hit-and-run cases.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Forensic tools</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cellebrite UFED</span><span class="dval">Industry standard for mobile extraction — extracts data from locked phones, deleted messages, app data.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">EnCase</span><span class="dval">Computer/disk forensics — creates forensic images and generates court-ready reports.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">FTK</span><span class="dval">Forensic Toolkit — parses hard drives, rebuilds deleted files, indexes all text for keyword search.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Oxygen Forensic</span><span class="dval">Specialised in cloud and app data — extracts Instagram DMs, Telegram messages, Google account data.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Order of volatility</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Rule</span><span class="dval">Collect the most volatile (quickly disappearing) data first.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Order</span><span class="dval">RAM → CPU cache → swap files → running processes → network connections → disk → removable media</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Why it matters</span><span class="dval">RAM contains encryption keys, active sessions, and passwords — all lost when device powers off.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In a sextortion case in Kerala, police seized the accused's Android phone. Using Cellebrite, they recovered 340 deleted video files proving the honey-trap operation — even after a factory reset. The IMEI matched CDR records confirming it was his device.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Cellebrite's tools can extract data from over 10,000 mobile device profiles. In India, Cellebrite is used by CBI, state forensic labs, and major state cyber cells.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">🔍</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Subscriber Information</div><div class="card-sub">Identity data linked to telecom/internet accounts</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Personal details held by telecom operators or ISPs for every SIM or internet account — name, address, KYC documents — linked to a mobile number or IP address.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_ev_subscriber"><span class="arr" id="arr_ev_subscriber">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_ev_subscriber"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What it includes</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Personal data</span><span class="dval">Full legal name, residential address, date of birth, photograph.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">KYC documents</span><span class="dval">Aadhaar, PAN card, passport, or voter ID used at SIM activation.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Account details</span><span class="dval">Mobile number, SIM activation date, connection type (prepaid/postpaid), email ID.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMSI &amp; IMEI</span><span class="dval">IMSI (SIM identity) and IMEI (device identity) linked to the subscriber.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How investigators obtain it</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal process</span><span class="dval">Police issue a notice under Section 91 CrPC (Section 94 BNSS) to the TSP/ISP.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Urgent cases</span><span class="dval">For financial fraud or terrorism, requests go directly to the TSP's nodal officer.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Court order</span><span class="dval">For ISP-held IP-to-subscriber mapping, a magistrate order may be needed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Timeline</span><span class="dval">TSPs must respond within 7 days under TRAI; nodal officers handle urgent requests within 24 hrs.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fake KYC</span><span class="dval">Fraudsters use forged Aadhaar or purchased SIMs — subscriber data may not lead to real accused.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bulk SIM farms</span><span class="dval">Organised fraud groups use hundreds of SIMs bought with fake IDs from vulnerable populations.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">OTT services</span><span class="dval">WhatsApp and Telegram store minimal encrypted subscriber data — may require MLAT requests.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A victim received fraud calls from a number registered to a daily-wage worker in Bihar who had never owned a smartphone. Investigation revealed a SIM farm — hundreds of SIMs activated using the worker's Aadhaar without his knowledge, sold to a cyber fraud gang in Jamtara.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Jamtara, Jharkhand became infamous as India's 'phishing capital' — gangs used fake SIMs and simple scripts to commit crores in OTP fraud. The Netflix series 'Jamtara' is based on this.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">📞</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">CDR</div><div class="card-sub">Call Detail Record</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">A computerised log maintained by every telecom operator recording metadata of all voice calls and SMS — who called whom, when, for how long, and from where.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_ev_cdr"><span class="arr" id="arr_ev_cdr">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_ev_cdr"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What CDR contains</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Call info</span><span class="dval">Originating number (A party), receiving number (B party), call start time, duration, call type.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Location</span><span class="dval">Cell tower ID (CGI) used during the call — triangulated to pinpoint approximate location.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMEI</span><span class="dval">Device IMEI linked to the SIM — crucial when suspects swap SIMs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Roaming</span><span class="dval">Roaming info if subscriber was in another circle — helps track movement across states.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types of CDR requests</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Targeted CDR</span><span class="dval">All calls made/received by a specific number over a time range.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Tower dump</span><span class="dval">All numbers active on a specific cell tower during a specific time window — used in kidnapping and murder cases.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMEI-based CDR</span><span class="dval">Track all SIMs ever used in a specific device — useful when SIM is discarded but phone is retained.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Reverse lookup</span><span class="dval">Find all calls to/from a victim's number — identifies unknown callers.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal aspects</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Retention</span><span class="dval">TRAI mandates telecom operators retain CDR for minimum 1 year.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Request process</span><span class="dval">Senior police officer (DSP and above) can request CDR directly from telecom nodal officers.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Admissibility</span><span class="dval">CDR requires a Section 65B certificate for court submission.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In the 2012 Delhi gang rape case, CDR analysis of all phones near the crime scene and the bus route helped police reconstruct movements of all six accused. Tower dump data from three towers narrowed down suspects within hours.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>A single CDR request can return thousands of records. Investigators use tools like i2 Analyst's Notebook or Maltego to visualise call networks and identify 'hub' numbers in a fraud gang.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(34,197,94,0.10)">🌐</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">IPDR</div><div class="card-sub">Internet Protocol Detail Record</div><span class="badge badge-green">Evidence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The internet counterpart of CDR — every ISP/TSP logs all data sessions: source IP, destination IP, ports, protocol, data volume, and timestamps. Critical for tracing internet-based crimes.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_ev_ipdr"><span class="arr" id="arr_ev_ipdr">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_ev_ipdr"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What IPDR contains</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Session data</span><span class="dval">Source IP, destination IP, source port, destination port, protocol (TCP/UDP), timestamp.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Volume</span><span class="dval">Amount of data uploaded/downloaded per session — can indicate large file transfers.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Application</span><span class="dval">Some IPDRs include app-layer info (WhatsApp, YouTube) under deep packet inspection.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">NAT port</span><span class="dval">In CGNAT environments, port number uniquely identifies the subscriber sharing a public IP.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How it is used</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IP-to-person mapping</span><span class="dval">Police get IP from server log → request IPDR from ISP → identify which subscriber used that IP at that time.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Transaction trail</span><span class="dval">Identify which internet connection was used to perform a fraudulent UPI transaction or email hack.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cross-verification</span><span class="dval">Correlate IPDR with CDR — verify if same person was on a data session and call simultaneously.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Dark web</span><span class="dval">If suspect accessed .onion sites via Tor, IPDR shows encrypted sessions to known Tor entry nodes.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal aspects</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal basis</span><span class="dval">Obtained under Section 69 IT Act / Section 91 CrPC (Section 94 BNSS) notice to ISP.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CGNAT challenge</span><span class="dval">Thousands of users share one public IP — port number in IPDR is essential to identify one subscriber.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Retention</span><span class="dval">DoT guidelines require ISPs to retain IPDR for 1 year.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A ransomware attack hit a Mumbai hospital. The server log showed attacker IP: 103.45.XX.XX. Police served an IPDR request to the ISP, which mapped that IP and port to a specific broadband subscriber in Thane. Combined with CDR, they confirmed the suspect was home during the attack.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>The Supreme Court and High Courts have upheld IPDR as admissible evidence in India when combined with proper 65B certification.</p></div></div></div></div></div>
  <div id="sec-intelligence" class="section"><div class="grid" id="grid-intelligence"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🔎</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">OSINT</div><div class="card-sub">Open Source Intelligence</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Intelligence collected from publicly available sources — social media, websites, domain records, satellite imagery, public databases, and dark web forums — without any covert access.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_intel_osint"><span class="arr" id="arr_intel_osint">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_intel_osint"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Sources</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Social media</span><span class="dval">Facebook, Instagram, X (Twitter), LinkedIn — profile photos, check-ins, employer info, relationships.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">WHOIS &amp; DNS</span><span class="dval">Domain registration records, nameservers, IP history — reveals who registered a phishing domain.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Shodan</span><span class="dval">Search engine for internet-connected devices — finds exposed servers, cameras, industrial systems.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Dark web</span><span class="dval">Forums like BreachForums — leaked databases, advertised malware, criminal profiles.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Geo-intelligence</span><span class="dval">Google Maps, satellite images, Wikimapia — verify locations claimed by suspects.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">OSINT tools</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Maltego</span><span class="dval">Visual link analysis — maps relationships between domains, IPs, emails, and social profiles.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">SpiderFoot</span><span class="dval">Automated OSINT scanner — discovers emails, subdomains, breached credentials linked to a target.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">theHarvester</span><span class="dval">Collects emails, hostnames, IPs associated with a domain from search engines.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Google Dorks</span><span class="dval">Advanced search operators (site:, filetype:, intitle:) to find exposed data on public websites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Reverse image</span><span class="dval">TinEye or Google Images — identify fake profile photos stolen from another person.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal &amp; ethical limits</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Allowed</span><span class="dval">Viewing publicly shared content, domain records, open government data, public social media profiles.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">NOT allowed</span><span class="dval">Creating fake profiles to befriend a suspect, hacking, or accessing private data without authorisation.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Documentation</span><span class="dval">Screenshots with timestamps and URLs; archive web pages using HTTrack as evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Kolkata woman was blackmailed by someone claiming to have her private photos. Investigators found the blackmailer's WhatsApp profile photo on a Nigerian Instagram account. Reverse image search led to a network of 12 fake profiles used for honey-trap operations across India.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>OSINT was central to identifying perpetrators of the 2021 Colonial Pipeline ransomware attack. Analysts traced Bitcoin wallets in the ransom note to DarkSide's infrastructure through blockchain OSINT.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🕵️</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">HUMINT</div><div class="card-sub">Human Intelligence</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Intelligence gathered from human sources — informants, undercover officers, interviews with suspects and victims, and inter-agency cooperation — as opposed to technical or open-source methods.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_intel_humint"><span class="arr" id="arr_intel_humint">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_intel_humint"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Methods</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Informants</span><span class="dval">Recruited individuals inside criminal networks who provide tips in exchange for protection or money.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Undercover ops</span><span class="dval">Officers infiltrate online criminal forums posing as buyers, hackers, or money mules.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Interviews</span><span class="dval">Structured questioning of victims, witnesses, and arrested suspects to extract operational details.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Debriefing</span><span class="dval">Arrested cybercriminals cooperate in exchange for plea deals — revealing gang structure, scripts, bank accounts.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Inter-agency</span><span class="dval">Coordination between CBI, state police, CERT-In, banks, and international agencies (Interpol, FBI).</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">HUMINT in cyber crime</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Forum infiltration</span><span class="dval">Officers join dark web forums posing as buyers to identify sellers of malware, stolen cards, or drugs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Call centre raids</span><span class="dval">HUMINT from disgruntled employees exposes fake tech-support or loan-app fraud call centres.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Courier networks</span><span class="dval">Money mule recruiters operate physical courier networks — informants inside logistics companies help.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cross-border</span><span class="dval">Interpol's Gateway programme and bilateral MLAT agreements are formal HUMINT cooperation channels.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cover maintenance</span><span class="dval">Undercover officers must maintain credibility over months without breaking cover.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal constraints</span><span class="dval">Evidence from undercover operations must comply with entrapment laws — officer cannot induce a crime.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Source reliability</span><span class="dval">Informants may have personal agendas; information must be corroborated with technical evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>In Operation Chakra-II (2023), CBI and state police used HUMINT from informants inside telecom stores plus OSINT to dismantle a network handling over 1 crore fraudulent SIM cards. HUMINT from an arrested mule revealed the gang's operator in Rajasthan.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Europol's 2023 IOCTA report credits HUMINT-based infiltration of dark web forums as the primary method for identifying ransomware-as-a-service administrators — technical attribution alone is not sufficient.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🎭</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Threat Actor</div><div class="card-sub">Entity responsible for a cyber attack</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Any individual, group, or nation-state that intentionally carries out malicious cyber activities — from bored teenagers to state-sponsored operatives — categorised by motive, capability, and sophistication.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_intel_threat-actor"><span class="arr" id="arr_intel_threat-actor">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_intel_threat-actor"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Script kiddies</span><span class="dval">Low-skill individuals using pre-written tools without understanding them. Motive: thrill/attention.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Hacktivists</span><span class="dval">Groups like Anonymous hacking for political/social causes — DDoS attacks, data leaks.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cybercriminals</span><span class="dval">Financially motivated individuals or organised gangs — ransomware, BEC, card fraud, phishing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Insider threats</span><span class="dval">Disgruntled employees, contractors, or bribed insiders misusing legitimate access.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Nation-state APTs</span><span class="dval">Government-backed threat groups — Lazarus (North Korea), APT28 (Russia), APT41 (China).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cyber mercenaries</span><span class="dval">Hack-for-hire groups like NSO Group (Pegasus) working for paying clients.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Profiling an actor</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">TTPs</span><span class="dval">Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures — the actor's unique modus operandi, mapped using MITRE ATT&amp;CK.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Infrastructure</span><span class="dval">C2 server IP ranges, hosting providers, domain patterns, SSL certificate reuse.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Malware signatures</span><span class="dval">Custom malware carries code artifacts, strings, or compile paths that identify the developer.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Timing patterns</span><span class="dval">Attack timestamps reveal the actor's timezone and work schedule.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">What it is</span><span class="dval">Globally accepted knowledge base of adversary tactics based on real-world observations.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Use in India</span><span class="dval">Used by CERT-In, NIC, and large banks to map attacks to known threat actors.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">14 tactics</span><span class="dval">Reconnaissance → Initial Access → Execution → Persistence → Privilege Escalation → … → Exfiltration → Impact</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>The 2020 Mumbai power grid outage was linked by Recorded Future to RedEcho, a suspected Chinese state-sponsored APT group, based on infrastructure overlaps and TTPs matching previous Chinese cyber operations.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>North Korea's Lazarus Group is estimated to have stolen over $3 billion in cryptocurrency between 2017–2023 — funding the country's weapons programme. They are the most prolific financially motivated nation-state actor.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🧬</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Attribution</div><div class="card-sub">Identifying the perpetrator of a cyber attack</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The complex process of linking a cyber attack to a specific person, group, or nation-state using technical indicators, behavioural analysis, OSINT, HUMINT, and legal-grade evidence.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_intel_attribution"><span class="arr" id="arr_intel_attribution">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_intel_attribution"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Levels of attribution</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Technical</span><span class="dval">Lowest level — identifies IP, domain, malware hash. Easily spoofed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Operational</span><span class="dval">Links multiple incidents through shared infrastructure, TTPs, and timing — more reliable.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Strategic</span><span class="dval">Identifies actor's long-term goals, geopolitical context, motive — involves intelligence agencies.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Legal</span><span class="dval">Highest standard — proof beyond reasonable doubt required for criminal prosecution.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Technical indicators</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IP &amp; domain history</span><span class="dval">Passive DNS, WHOIS history, Shodan — has this IP been used in previous attacks?</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Malware analysis</span><span class="dval">Code similarity, compile-time timezone, hardcoded strings, mutex names, PDB paths.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">C2 infrastructure</span><span class="dval">Reuse of hosting providers, SSL certificates, URL patterns across campaigns.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Victimology</span><span class="dval">Who was targeted? Nation-state actors target specific sectors/countries, revealing motive.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">False flags</span><span class="dval">Sophisticated actors plant evidence pointing to another group.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">VPN / Tor / proxies</span><span class="dval">Multi-hop anonymisation makes IP-based attribution unreliable.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Shared tools</span><span class="dval">Multiple groups use Cobalt Strike or Metasploit — tool-based attribution alone is insufficient.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Speed vs accuracy</span><span class="dval">Intelligence agencies attribute quickly for policy; legal prosecution requires far stronger evidence.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>The 2021 SolarWinds attack was attributed to APT29 (Russia's SVR) based on code overlap with previous Turla malware, Eastern European infrastructure, US government victimology, and timestamps consistent with Moscow business hours.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Only about 20% of cyber attacks result in successful legal attribution (arrest + conviction). Nation-state attacks almost never result in prosecution — geopolitics prevent extradition.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(245,158,11,0.10)">🔌</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Takedown</div><div class="card-sub">Disabling malicious online infrastructure</div><span class="badge badge-amber">Intelligence</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The coordinated action of disabling, seizing, or disrupting malicious online infrastructure — phishing sites, C2 servers, botnets, or dark web marketplaces — through legal orders or technical means.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_intel_takedown"><span class="arr" id="arr_intel_takedown">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_intel_takedown"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Domain suspension</span><span class="dval">Registrar suspends domain after abuse complaint or court order — most common for phishing sites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Server seizure</span><span class="dval">Physical or virtual server seized by law enforcement — data preserved as evidence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">ISP blocking</span><span class="dval">ISP instructed to block access to malicious IP/domain within their network.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Sink-holing</span><span class="dval">DNS of botnet's C2 domain redirected to law enforcement server — bots connect harmlessly.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Dark web takedown</span><span class="dval">Coordinated international operation seizes servers hosting illegal marketplace.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Process in India</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CERT-In</span><span class="dval">Issues advisory to hosting providers and ISPs — typically within 4 hours for critical sites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Court order</span><span class="dval">For persistent sites, police obtain court order under IT Act Section 69A.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">DOT blocking</span><span class="dval">Department of Telecom can block at network level — used for fraud, terror-related sites.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">International</span><span class="dval">India uses Interpol channels, MLAT, or direct coordination with APNIC/ICANN for foreign-hosted sites.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Challenges</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bulletproof hosting</span><span class="dval">Providers in certain jurisdictions that ignore abuse complaints.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fast-flux DNS</span><span class="dval">Malware rapidly changes IP addresses — traditional domain blocking is ineffective.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Hydra effect</span><span class="dval">When one site is taken down, five more appear — requires sustained disruption.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>Operation Endgame (May 2024, Europol) was the largest-ever botnet takedown — simultaneously disrupting Smokeloader, IcedID, Trickbot, and SystemBC across 100+ servers in Europe and North America. Over 2,000 domains were sink-holed.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Silk Road was taken down by FBI in 2013. Its founder was identified partly because he posted about the site using his real email on a public forum — a classic OSINT failure.</p></div></div></div></div></div>
  <div id="sec-crimes" class="section"><div class="grid" id="grid-crimes"><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">💸</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Money Mule</div><div class="card-sub">Recruited fund launderer for cyber criminals</div><span class="badge badge-red">Financial crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">A person — often unsuspecting — who receives stolen money into their bank account and transfers it onward minus a commission. They form the crucial cash-out layer in almost every financial cyber crime.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_cr_mule"><span class="arr" id="arr_cr_mule">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_cr_mule"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How mules are recruited</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Fake job ads</span><span class="dval">Ads for 'payment processor', 'financial agent', or 'remote work' — no skills required, high pay promised.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Social media DMs</span><span class="dval">Instagram/WhatsApp messages offering part-time income for 'helping process payments'.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Romance scams</span><span class="dval">Romantic partner built online over months gradually convinces victim to receive/forward money.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Loan app fraud</span><span class="dval">Victims in debt coerced into mule activity to 'repay loans' to fraudulent apps.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How the operation works</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Layer 1</span><span class="dval">Fraud proceeds enter mule's account (usually within minutes of the scam).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Transfer</span><span class="dval">Mule transfers to another mule account or crypto exchange — keeping 5–20% commission.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Layering</span><span class="dval">Multiple mules used in sequence to distance money from origin.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Cash out</span><span class="dval">Final mule withdraws cash, buys crypto, or purchases gift cards for the gang.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Legal consequences</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">India</span><span class="dval">Prosecution under IPC 420 (cheating), PMLA, and IT Act Section 66.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Sentence</span><span class="dval">PMLA conviction carries 3–7 years imprisonment and attachment of property.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">No ignorance defence</span><span class="dval">Receiving suspicious funds and forwarding them constitutes wilful blindness — ignorance is not a full defence.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Account freeze</span><span class="dval">Innocent mule accounts are frozen — victim loses access to own funds for months.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A 22-year-old engineering student in Pune responded to a WhatsApp job ad offering ₹500 per transfer. He forwarded ₹4.2 lakh across 3 transactions. Police traced fraud proceeds to his account via IPDR. He was arrested under PMLA despite claiming ignorance, and his account was frozen for 18 months.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>Europol's 2023 EMMA operation identified 10,000 money mules across Europe in a single month-long operation.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">₿</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Cryptocurrency Tracing</div><div class="card-sub">Blockchain forensic analysis to follow illicit funds</div><span class="badge badge-red">Financial crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">The discipline of tracking cryptocurrency transactions across public blockchains to identify wallets, exchanges, and the real-world identities behind illicit funds — despite the pseudonymous nature of crypto.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_cr_crypto"><span class="arr" id="arr_cr_crypto">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_cr_crypto"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Why crypto is traceable</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Public ledger</span><span class="dval">Every Bitcoin/Ethereum transaction is permanently recorded on a public blockchain — anyone can view it.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Pseudonymous</span><span class="dval">Wallets are addresses with no name, but all transactions are visible. Link address to identity = game over.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Clustering</span><span class="dval">If two addresses fund the same transaction, they are likely controlled by the same person.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Exchange KYC</span><span class="dval">When funds reach a regulated exchange (Binance, WazirX), KYC data links wallet to real person.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Obfuscation &amp; counters</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Mixers/tumblers</span><span class="dval">Pools users' crypto and returns different coins — breaks direct transaction trail.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Coinjoin</span><span class="dval">Multiple users combine transactions to obscure who paid whom.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Chain-hopping</span><span class="dval">Convert Bitcoin → Monero → Ethereum across chains to obscure trail.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Monero</span><span class="dval">Privacy coin — transactions not publicly visible. Much harder to trace.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Counter tools</span><span class="dval">Chainalysis Reactor can de-mix transactions using statistical analysis and exchange data.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Investigative tools</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Chainalysis Reactor</span><span class="dval">Used by CBI, ED, and major international agencies — visualises transaction flows across blockchains.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Elliptic Navigator</span><span class="dval">Strong on DeFi and NFT fraud tracing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CipherTrace</span><span class="dval">Acquired by Mastercard — used for exchange compliance and law enforcement.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Open source</span><span class="dval">Blockchair, OXT.me, Etherscan — free block explorers for basic transaction tracing.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>After the 2022 Bitfinex hack ($4.5B in Bitcoin stolen in 2016), IRS-CI and DOJ traced funds through 12 layers of mixers over 6 years using Chainalysis. The suspects were arrested when funds finally moved to a KYC exchange.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>The US DOJ seized over $1.4 billion in Bitcoin from the Bitfinex hack — the largest financial seizure in DOJ history. The blockchain's permanent ledger meant 6-year-old transactions were still fully traceable.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">📱</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">SIM Swap Fraud</div><div class="card-sub">Unauthorised mobile number porting to a criminal's SIM</div><span class="badge badge-red">Telecom crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Fraudsters convince or bribe a telecom outlet to port the victim's mobile number to a new SIM they control — giving them access to all OTPs, calls, and 2FA messages linked to the number.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_cr_simswap"><span class="arr" id="arr_cr_simswap">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_cr_simswap"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How it happens</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">PII collection</span><span class="dval">Attacker collects victim's personal details from data breaches or phishing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Outlet approach</span><span class="dval">Attacker visits a telecom store with forged ID or uses a corrupt insider to request a duplicate SIM.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bypass verification</span><span class="dval">Agent asks KYC questions; attacker answers using stolen PII — 'What is your registered address?'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">SIM activated</span><span class="dval">New SIM issued; victim's original SIM deactivated. Attacker receives all calls and OTPs.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Bank access</span><span class="dval">Attacker logs into net banking with stolen credentials + OTP from new SIM → transfers all funds.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Warning signs for victims</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Signal loss</span><span class="dval">Phone suddenly shows 'No network' or 'SIM not registered' — first and most critical sign.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Missed bank alerts</span><span class="dval">Stop receiving OTPs or bank SMS — attacker is receiving them.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Unusual calls</span><span class="dval">Incoming calls asking you to confirm OTPs you didn't request.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Immediate action</span><span class="dval">Call your telecom operator on another phone immediately; ask for SIM freeze. Call bank to freeze account.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Investigative approach</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CDR analysis</span><span class="dval">Check which tower new SIM connected to first — locates where the fraudster activated it.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Outlet CCTV</span><span class="dval">Most outlets have CCTV — footage of person who requested the duplicate SIM.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Outlet records</span><span class="dval">Biometric/signature records of who submitted the duplicate SIM request.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Insider angle</span><span class="dval">If insider is involved, CDR between insider and attacker is traced via call records.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Mumbai stock broker lost ₹1.6 crore in 4 hours after a SIM swap. His number was ported after an attacker visited a franchise with a forged Aadhaar. The attacker used stolen net banking credentials + OTPs from the new SIM to make 8 transfers to mule accounts.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>RBI's 2023 Annual Report listed SIM swap fraud as one of the top 3 banking fraud methods in India. TRAI has since mandated a 7-day cooling period for SIM re-issuance.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">⚠️</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">Sextortion</div><div class="card-sub">Online sexual blackmail using intimate content</div><span class="badge badge-red">Sexual crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">Criminals use real or fabricated intimate images/videos to blackmail victims for money or more content — often starting with a honey-trap on social media or dating apps.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_cr_sextortion"><span class="arr" id="arr_cr_sextortion">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_cr_sextortion"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Common methods</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Honey-trap</span><span class="dval">Fake attractive profile befriends target → builds trust → convinces to video call → records compromising content.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Malware variant</span><span class="dval">Victim's device infected with RAT → webcam silently activated → footage captured.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Non-consensual</span><span class="dval">Ex-partner or acquaintance threatens to share real intimate content unless demands are met.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">AI deepfake</span><span class="dval">Attacker uses victim's social media photos to create deepfake intimate content — no real contact needed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Teen targeting</span><span class="dval">Targets minors on gaming platforms — grooming leads to obtaining images, then blackmail.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Demands &amp; escalation</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Financial</span><span class="dval">Money via UPI, crypto, or gift cards — typically ₹5,000 to ₹5 lakh.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">More content</span><span class="dval">Demands escalate — victim pressured to send more images to 'buy silence'.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Paying backfires</span><span class="dval">Payment almost always leads to more demands — attacker knows victim will comply.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">What victims should do</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Do NOT pay</span><span class="dval">Payment escalates demands and confirms victim will comply.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Preserve evidence</span><span class="dval">Screenshot all conversations, note profile links, record UPI/account details.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Report</span><span class="dval">cybercrime.gov.in → 'Women/Child related crime' → Sextortion. Also call 1930.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Platform report</span><span class="dval">Report the account — most platforms have emergency removal for non-consensual intimate images (NCII).</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Support</span><span class="dval">Contact iCall (9152987821) or Vandrevala Foundation for psychological support.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A 17-year-old student in Chennai was contacted by a 'girl' on Instagram. After weeks of conversation she was convinced to video call. The call was recorded and she was threatened unless she paid ₹20,000. She reported to cybercrime.gov.in; police traced the gang to Rajasthan running 50+ simultaneous operations.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>NCRB data shows sextortion cases in India grew 75% between 2020–2022. Bharatpur, Rajasthan became known as the 'sextortion capital' — police have dismantled dozens of gangs there.</p></div></div></div><div class="card"><div class="card-header"><div class="card-top"><div class="card-icon" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,0.10)">📧</div><div class="card-meta"><div class="card-title">BEC</div><div class="card-sub">Business Email Compromise</div><span class="badge badge-red">Corporate crime</span></div></div></div><div class="card-body">A sophisticated scam targeting businesses — attackers compromise or spoof a corporate email to deceive employees, vendors, or clients into transferring money or sharing sensitive data.</div><div class="expand-btn" id="btn_cr_bec"><span class="arr" id="arr_cr_bec">▼</span>&nbsp;Tap to expand</div><div class="detail-wrap" id="dp_cr_bec"><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Types of BEC</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">CEO fraud</span><span class="dval">Attacker impersonates the CEO and emails CFO: 'Transfer ₹50 lakh urgently — confidential deal.'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Invoice fraud</span><span class="dval">Attacker spoofs a vendor's email: 'Our bank account has changed — please send payment to new account.'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Attorney impersonation</span><span class="dval">Fake law firm email claims victim owes money or must act urgently on a legal matter.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">HR/W-2 fraud</span><span class="dval">Attacker impersonates employee to HR: 'Please update my salary transfer to this new account.'</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Real estate</span><span class="dval">Buyer receives spoofed email from 'lawyer' with new bank account for property down payment.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">How attackers gain access</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Phishing</span><span class="dval">Credential-harvesting email tricks employee into entering password on a fake login page.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Credential stuffing</span><span class="dval">Breached password from another site tried on corporate email — works if employees reuse passwords.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">IMAP brute force</span><span class="dval">Automated attack guesses email password — especially on accounts without MFA enabled.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Email spoofing</span><span class="dval">Attacker forges the 'From' field to look like CEO — no actual hack needed.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Domain lookalike</span><span class="dval">Registers company-rnail.com (rn looks like m) — victim doesn't notice the difference.</span></div></div><div class="dsec"><div class="dsec-title">Red flags &amp; prevention</div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Urgency + secrecy</span><span class="dval">Any email demanding urgent wire transfer with 'keep this confidential' is a major red flag.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Reply-to mismatch</span><span class="dval">Display name says 'CEO' but reply-to is ceo@company-rnail.com — always check the actual email address.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Verification</span><span class="dval">Call the sender on a known number to verify any payment instruction.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">MFA</span><span class="dval">Enable multi-factor authentication on all corporate email accounts.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">DMARC/SPF</span><span class="dval">Configure email authentication protocols to prevent domain spoofing.</span></div><div class="drow"><span class="dlabel">Recovery</span><span class="dval">Report to cybercrime.gov.in immediately; call 1930; contact bank to issue SWIFT recall.</span></div></div><div class="example-box"><div class="ex-title">📍 Real-World Example</div><p>A Hyderabad manufacturing company received an email from its Singapore supplier saying their bank account had changed. Finance transferred $85,000 to the new account. Investigation revealed the supplier's email was hacked 3 weeks earlier; the attacker waited silently until a large invoice was expected.</p></div><div class="fact-box"><div class="fact-title">💡 Did You Know?</div><p>FBI IC3 data: BEC caused $2.9 billion in losses in the USA alone in 2023 — the single highest-loss cybercrime category, far exceeding ransomware. Globally, BEC losses are estimated at $50+ billion since 2013.</p></div></div></div></div></div>

  <div id="sec-flow" class="section">
    <div class="flow-section">
      <h3>🔍 Standard Cyber Crime Investigation Pipeline</h3>
      <div class="flow-wrap">
        <div class="flow">
          <div class="fstep danger">1. Report<div class="fsub">FIR / Portal</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">2. Register<div class="fsub">Case number</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep info">3. Collect<div class="fsub">Digital evidence</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep info">4. Analyse<div class="fsub">CDR / IPDR</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">5. Identify<div class="fsub">OSINT / HUMINT</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">6. Attribute<div class="fsub">Threat actor</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep success">7. Act<div class="fsub">Takedown / Arrest</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="note-bar">⚠️ Each stage may loop back — new evidence at step 4 can open fresh leads requiring additional collection or even a fresh FIR.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-section">
      <h3>💸 Money Trail in Financial Cyber Crime</h3>
      <div class="flow-wrap">
        <div class="flow">
          <div class="fstep danger">Victim<div class="fsub">Funds stolen</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">Money Mule<div class="fsub">Layer 1</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">Mule 2 / 3<div class="fsub">Layering</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">Crypto Mixer<div class="fsub">Obfuscation</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">Exchange<div class="fsub">Cashout</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep success">Tracing<div class="fsub">Blockchain</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="note-bar">🔗 Despite mixing, blockchain transactions are permanent and public — investigators can trace funds across years using tools like Chainalysis Reactor.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-section">
      <h3>📱 SIM Swap Attack Flow</h3>
      <div class="flow-wrap">
        <div class="flow">
          <div class="fstep">PII Collection<div class="fsub">Phishing / breach</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep danger">Telecom Outlet<div class="fsub">Fake ID / insider</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep danger">SIM Ported<div class="fsub">Victim goes dark</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep danger">OTP Intercepted<div class="fsub">Bank access</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep">Funds Transfer<div class="fsub">To mule accounts</div></div>
          <div class="farr">→</div>
          <div class="fstep success">Investigate<div class="fsub">CDR + CCTV</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

